Z||000013||GOLDSE||09-21-2001||02:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by NEPTUNE:<BR>[B]Did ISUZU impulse also have that "feature"?[B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, the Impulse had a single wiper arm.  Used to own an 85 Impulse.  The other neat thing about that car, was that it had an automatic climate control.  Ahead of it's time for a small economical car.<P>The most current issue of Super Rod magazine has a feature on an early Camaro that was/is mostly hand fabricated, with the single wiper arm feature.  The wiper and mechanicals are from a late Mercedes.  A lot of work went into that.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||btoth||09-21-2001||03:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SLYGUY:<BR><B>Mercedes-Benz does it with most of their sedans. <P>Very complicated mechanicals with this. The blade actually REACHES towards the center of the windshield as it swings, and retracts to a shorter length as it wipes the passengers side of the windshield. Then it does the same on the way back. <P>You could wreck into the car watching the wiper do this as it is driving towards you.<BR>Really cool.<P>No, I haven't seen it done either. Closest I have seen on a non MB is someone put a huge wiperblade from a van or an SUV or something. Doesn't work as well I can only imagine, but looks pretty cool.<P>Just my $.02<P>Sly</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Our Audi has 2 blades (of course), but the tip of the passenger-side wiper curls toward the driver-side of the car when it goes as far as it can... pretty weird, but cool at the same time.||||reg||1
